  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-forecast essay arguing that the agentic AI threat is not hype but arrives through two ordinary, compounding capability curves: agent *endurance* (METR's finding that autonomous task length doubles every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one orchestrator coordinating dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure of Claude Code used across ~30 espionage targets). The core thesis is that threat scales as duration × breadth — a product that outpaces linearly-scaling human security review — and that attackers bypass cost/access gates entirely via \"LLMjacking\" (stolen cloud credentials running AI workloads billed to the victim, ~$46k–$100k+/day), severing the industry's assumption that the capability user is the payer. A recurring framing device is the *Terminator* franchise: the robot's menace was \"absolutely will not stop,\" now reinterpreted as \"will not stop billing,\" with Skynet-as-distributed-system mirroring orchestrator architectures. The tone is a pointed, well-researched argument aimed at engineering/security teams skeptical of agentic-risk claims, using probability math (1 − (1−p)^n) to show swarms only need to succeed once.",
